  Clarification on Stale Epoch Handling

  Based on our code analysis of LeaderEpochFileCache.endOffsetFor(), a stale/old epoch (even one that is no longer in the cache) should NOT cause UNDEFINED_EPOCH_OFFSET. Here's why:

  Map.Entry<Integer, EpochEntry> higherEntry = epochs.higherEntry(requestedEpoch);
  if (higherEntry == null) {
      // UNDEFINED - only when requestedEpoch >= all known epochs
      return (UNDEFINED_EPOCH, UNDEFINED_EPOCH_OFFSET);
  } else {
      Map.Entry<Integer, EpochEntry> floorEntry = epochs.floorEntry(requestedEpoch);
      if (floorEntry == null) {
          // requestedEpoch < earliestKnownEpoch
          // Returns VALID response pointing to next epoch's start
          return (requestedEpoch, higherEntry.getValue().startOffset());
      }
      // ... normal case
  }

  Example - Your Scenario:
  - Consumer committed with epoch 5
  - Retention deleted old segments, epoch cache now only has [7, 8, 9]
  - Consumer requests validation for epoch 5

  What happens:
  - epochs.higherEntry(5) → returns epoch 7 ✓ (exists, because 7 > 5)
  - epochs.floorEntry(5) → returns null (nothing ≤ 5)
  - Broker returns (epoch=5, endOffset=startOfEpoch7) — valid response, NOT UNDEFINED

  When UNDEFINED_EPOCH_OFFSET actually occurs:
  - Only when higherEntry == null
  - This means requestedEpoch ≥ latestKnownEpoch
  - i.e., the epoch is too NEW, not too old
  - OR the epoch cache is completely empty

  The implication:
  If you're seeing behavior consistent with UNDEFINED_EPOCH_OFFSET (full reset instead of seek-to-truncation-point), the cause is likely NOT a stale epoch. It would have to be:
  1. Epoch cache is empty on the new leader, OR
  2. Some other issue we haven't identified

  Can you help us understand if there's something we're missing about your scenario?

